//! `bsc imprint` — the WRITTEN half of knowing who the user is.
//!
//! `bsc ear` learns the maintainer's VOICE. This asks the same question of text, with no microphone and
//! no camera: does this read like the person the app knows?
//!
//! # It decides nothing, and that is structural
//!
//! There is no verdict, no overall score, and no record of any comparison. `explain` is a pure read that
//! writes nothing at all.
//!
//! That is not caution to be relaxed later — it is the maintainer's own stated value made unbuildable
//! around. `benefit-of-the-doubt-not-chances`: **no suspicion ledger, ever** — a person who caused three
//! false alarms this week is not believed less on the fourth — and **ambiguity yields anonymity, not a
//! guess**. A profile that accumulated mismatch evidence would be exactly that ledger, whether or not
//! anything read it. So the store holds a PROFILE of how someone writes, and nothing about any occasion
//! on which text failed to look like it.
//!
//! # Discriminative and descriptive are different kinds, and the type enforces it
//!
//! MEASURED 2026-09-08 over 2,535 typed messages / 60,185 words of the maintainer's own writing, against
//! the assistant's as a control:
//!
//! | dimension | maintainer | control | separation |
//! |---|---|---|---|
//! | sentence-start capitalisation | 0.049 | 0.993 | 2.00 SD |
//! | lowercase `i` | 0.966 | 0.003 | 1.99 SD |
//! | apostrophe omission | 0.336 | 0.002 | 1.94 SD |
//! | habitual misspellings | 0.429 | 0.012 | 1.01 SD |
//! | emoticons per 1k words | 4.58 | 2.95 | 0.71 SD |
//! | mean word length | 4.65 | 4.69 | 0.67 SD |
//!
//! **Averaging all six made things WORSE**: cross-author pairs closer than the worst same-author pair went
//! from 537/1764 to 1327/1764. Three near-perfect discriminators, averaged with two that are noise, get
//! dragged to the middle. So a dimension declares its [`Kind`], a comparison reads only the
//! discriminative ones, and the descriptive ones are reported because they are interesting — never
//! scored, because scoring them measurably destroys the signal.
//!
//! # Why a named vector and not an embedding
//!
//! The obvious alternative is a learned dense vector, the text twin of the ECAPA speaker embeddings the
//! voice half plans. Rejected for this, on the data: these markers are CATEGORICAL — the maintainer
//! essentially never capitalises and never spells `threshold` correctly — and an embedding blurs a
//! near-binary fact into a fraction of one dimension among hundreds. Worse, it can only ever report a
//! distance. A named vector can say WHICH dimension diverged, and an unexplainable number is precisely
//! what the no-ledger rule exists to prevent.
//!
//! Classical Burrows's Delta over function words was measured too, and separated only 1.37x here —
//! author and control write about identical subjects with identical jargon, which is the case function
//! words are supposed to survive and did not.
//!
//! # The corpus is part of the claim
//!
//! A [`Profile`] carries the corpus it was built from. A profile resting on 200 words and one resting on
//! 60,000 are not the same claim, and a reader must be able to tell them apart — so an under-evidenced
//! profile reports as ABSENT rather than as a confident zero, the house rule the comment harvest found
//! stated independently in fourteen records.

use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
use std::collections::BTreeMap;

/// What a dimension is FOR. The split is load-bearing — see the crate docs.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
#[serde(rename_all = "lowercase")]
pub enum Kind {
    /// Separates authors. Only these are ever compared.
    Discriminative,
    /// Interesting about a writer, but too weak to separate — reported, never scored.
    Descriptive,
}

/// One measured dimension of how someone writes.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct Dim {
    /// Stable machine name — the key a comparison reports against.
    pub name: String,
    /// What it means, in one line, so a report explains itself without this crate's source.
    pub about: String,
    pub kind: Kind,
    /// The measured value. A RATE or a RATIO in 0..1 unless the name says otherwise.
    pub value: f64,
    /// How many observations the value rests on. Zero means NOT MEASURED — never "measured as zero".
    pub n: u64,
}

impl Dim {
    /// Was there anything to measure? A dimension with no observations is ABSENT, and every reader
    /// must treat it as unknown rather than as its numeric value (which is a placeholder 0.0).
    pub fn measured(&self) -> bool {
        self.n > 0
    }
}

/// A measurement of one body of text: the named feature vector.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct Markers {
    pub dims: Vec<Dim>,
    /// Emoticons seen, most-used first — a VOCABULARY, not a rate. Kept beside the dims because
    /// which faces someone uses is more identifying than how often they use one.
    pub emoticons: Vec<(String, u64)>,
    /// Non-dictionary tokens that recur, with how often — the raw material habitual misspellings are
    /// found in. Reported, never scored: most of them are jargon.
    pub words: u64,
    pub messages: u64,
}

impl Markers {
    /// One dimension by name.
    pub fn get(&self, name: &str) -> Option<&Dim> {
        self.dims.iter().find(|d| d.name == name)
    }
    /// The dimensions a comparison may read. The ONLY way to obtain them — a caller cannot reach
    /// past this into the descriptive ones, which is what stops the averaging defect returning.
    pub fn discriminative(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = &Dim> {
        self.dims.iter().filter(|d| d.kind == Kind::Discriminative && d.measured())
    }
}

/// The dimension names, as consts so the CLI, the store and the tests cannot disagree about a key.
pub const CAP_START: &str = "cap_start_rate";
pub const LOWERCASE_I: &str = "lowercase_i_rate";
pub const APOS_OMIT: &str = "apos_omit_rate";
pub const HABIT_MISSPELL: &str = "habit_misspell_rate";
pub const EMOTICON_RATE: &str = "emoticons_per_1k";
pub const MEAN_WORD_LEN: &str = "mean_word_len";
pub const MEAN_MSG_WORDS: &str = "mean_message_words";

/// Contractions written WITHOUT the apostrophe. Deliberately a closed list: the general "word that
/// would take an apostrophe" test needs a dictionary, and the whole point of this dimension is the
/// HABIT, which shows in the common dozen.
const NO_APOS: &[&str] = &[
    "dont", "cant", "im", "lets", "wont", "didnt", "isnt", "thats", "whats", "youre", "theyre",
    "ive", "id", "ill", "wasnt", "arent", "couldnt", "shouldnt", "wouldnt", "hasnt", "havent",
];

/// A habitual misspelling and the correct form it displaces. The RATIO between them is the signal,
/// not the raw count: someone who writes `thresold` six times and `threshold` never is a different
/// claim from someone who does both.
///
/// This list is a SEED, not a closed set — the point of `bsc imprint learn` is to grow it from a
/// corpus rather than to guess it. These are the four the maintainer's own corpus produced.
pub const HABITS: &[(&str, &str)] = &[
    ("thresold", "threshold"),
    ("alot", "a lot"),
    ("nevermind", "never mind"),
    ("ect", "etc"),
];

/// The emoticon shapes recognised. Ordered longest-first so `>:d` is not eaten by `:d`.
const FACES: &[&str] = &[
    ">:d", ">:)", ">:(", "^^", "<3", "owo", "uwu", ":3", "x3", ":d", "xd", "xp", ":p", ":o", ":)",
    ":(", ":/", "=/", ":|", ";)", ";p", "8)", "83",
];

fn count_ci(hay: &str, needle: &str) -> u64 {
    hay.matches(needle).count() as u64
}

/// Whole-word occurrences of `w` in already-lowercased `hay`.
fn word_count(hay: &str, w: &str) -> u64 {
    let mut n = 0u64;
    let bytes = hay.as_bytes();
    let mut from = 0usize;
    while let Some(i) = hay[from..].find(w) {
        let s = from + i;
        let e = s + w.len();
        let left_ok = s == 0 || !(bytes[s - 1] as char).is_alphanumeric();
        let right_ok = e >= bytes.len() || !(bytes[e] as char).is_alphanumeric();
        if left_ok && right_ok {
            n += 1;
        }
        from = e.max(s + 1);
        if from >= hay.len() {
            break;
        }
    }
    n
}

/// Measure one body of text. `messages` are the separate things the person wrote — the split matters
/// because sentence-start capitalisation is measured per MESSAGE, and message length is a dimension.
///
/// PURE: no store, no clock, no filesystem. Every rule here is testable from a &str.
pub fn measure(messages: &[String]) -> Markers {
    let joined = messages.join("\n");
    let low = joined.to_lowercase();
    let words: Vec<&str> = low.split_whitespace().collect();
    let word_n = words.len() as u64;

    // Capitalisation is read from the first ALPHABETIC character of each message. A message opening
    // with a path, a flag or a quote says nothing about the habit and is skipped rather than counted
    // as lowercase — which would make anyone who pastes look like they never capitalise.
    let starts: Vec<char> =
        messages.iter().filter_map(|m| m.trim().chars().find(|c| c.is_alphabetic())).collect();
    let caps = starts.iter().filter(|c| c.is_uppercase()).count() as u64;

    // COUNTED ON THE ORIGINAL TEXT, not the lowercased copy — the first version of this counted
    // standalone `i` in `low`, which had already turned every `I` into an `i`, so a wholly-capitalised
    // writer measured as 100% lowercase. The dimension that separated best was the one silently
    // reading its own input backwards.
    let i_lower = word_count(&joined, "i");
    let i_upper = word_count(&joined, "I");
    let i_total = i_lower + i_upper;

    let no_apos: u64 = NO_APOS.iter().map(|w| word_count(&low, w)).sum();
    // Any word carrying an internal apostrophe — the other half of the ratio.
    let with_apos = low
        .split(|c: char| !(c.is_alphanumeric() || c == '\''))
        .filter(|w| w.len() > 2 && w.contains('\'') && !w.starts_with('\'') && !w.ends_with('\''))
        .count() as u64;

    let mut habit_bad = 0u64;
    let mut habit_good = 0u64;
    for (bad, good) in HABITS {
        habit_bad += word_count(&low, bad);
        habit_good += if good.contains(' ') { count_ci(&low, good) } else { word_count(&low, good) };
    }

    let mut faces: BTreeMap<String, u64> = BTreeMap::new();
    let mut scan = low.clone();
    for f in FACES {
        let n = count_ci(&scan, f);
        if n > 0 {
            faces.insert((*f).to_string(), n);
            // Remove what matched so a longer face is not re-counted by a shorter one it contains.
            scan = scan.replace(f, " ");
        }
    }
    let face_total: u64 = faces.values().sum();
    let mut emoticons: Vec<(String, u64)> = faces.into_iter().collect();
    emoticons.sort_by(|a, b| b.1.cmp(&a.1).then(a.0.cmp(&b.0)));

    let letters: u64 = words.iter().map(|w| w.chars().filter(|c| c.is_alphabetic()).count() as u64).sum();

    let rate = |num: u64, den: u64| if den == 0 { 0.0 } else { num as f64 / den as f64 };
    let dim = |name: &str, about: &str, kind: Kind, value: f64, n: u64| Dim {
        name: name.into(),
        about: about.into(),
        kind,
        value,
        n,
    };

    Markers {
        dims: vec![
            // The three that measured ~2 SD of separation. These carry the surface.
            dim(CAP_START, "share of messages whose first letter is capitalised", Kind::Discriminative,
                rate(caps, starts.len() as u64), starts.len() as u64),
            dim(LOWERCASE_I, "share of the first-person pronoun written lowercase", Kind::Discriminative,
                rate(i_lower, i_total), i_total),
            dim(APOS_OMIT, "share of contractions written without the apostrophe", Kind::Discriminative,
                rate(no_apos, no_apos + with_apos), no_apos + with_apos),
            // Weaker (1.01 SD) but categorical, and it strengthens as the habit list grows from use.
            dim(HABIT_MISSPELL, "share of known habitual misspellings against their correct forms",
                Kind::Discriminative, rate(habit_bad, habit_bad + habit_good), habit_bad + habit_good),
            // DESCRIPTIVE: measured at 0.71 and 0.67 SD. Interesting, and averaging them into a
            // comparison measurably made separation worse — so they are reported and never scored.
            dim(EMOTICON_RATE, "emoticons per 1000 words (the VOCABULARY above is the identifying part)",
                Kind::Descriptive, if word_n == 0 { 0.0 } else { 1000.0 * face_total as f64 / word_n as f64 },
                word_n),
            dim(MEAN_WORD_LEN, "mean alphabetic characters per word", Kind::Descriptive,
                rate(letters, word_n), word_n),
            dim(MEAN_MSG_WORDS, "mean words per message", Kind::Descriptive,
                rate(word_n, messages.len() as u64), messages.len() as u64),
        ],
        emoticons,
        words: word_n,
        messages: messages.len() as u64,
    }
}

/// How much text a profile needs before its dimensions are worth reading. Below this the profile
/// reports ABSENT rather than a confident number — the same rule the codebase states in fourteen
/// other places: an unmeasured thing must never present as its optimistic default.
pub const MIN_WORDS: u64 = 2_000;

/// An accumulated picture of how one person writes.
///
/// ACCUMULATES, never replaces — the maintainer's `imprint` semantics from the voice half: the app
/// LEARNS the user rather than enrolling them once, so more corpus only sharpens it.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct Profile {
    /// Whose. `me` is the only id anything writes today; the field exists so a second person is a
    /// new row rather than a schema change.
    pub id: String,
    pub markers: Markers,
    /// The BEHAVIOURAL half — when and where the person works, rather than how they write. Defaults
    /// to empty so a profile written before this facet reads as "not measured" rather than as a
    /// person who has never worked anywhere.
    #[serde(default)]
    pub behaviour: crate::behaviour::Behaviour,
    /// Every corpus folded in so far, newest last — what the profile RESTS on.
    pub sources: Vec<Source>,
    pub updated_at_ms: i64,
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct Source {
    pub label: String,
    pub messages: u64,
    pub words: u64,
    pub at_ms: i64,
}

impl Profile {
    /// Is there enough corpus for the dimensions to mean anything? A caller must ask this before
    /// reading a value — a profile built from one paragraph has numbers, and they are noise.
    pub fn evidenced(&self) -> bool {
        self.markers.words >= MIN_WORDS
    }
}

/// Fold a new measurement into a profile, weighting each dimension by the observations behind it.
///
/// Weighted, not averaged: a dimension measured over 4,000 observations and one measured over 12 are
/// not equal votes, and a plain mean would let a short message drag a well-evidenced habit.
pub fn accumulate(profile: &Markers, fresh: &Markers) -> Markers {
    let mut dims = Vec::with_capacity(profile.dims.len());
    for a in &profile.dims {
        let b = fresh.get(&a.name);
        let (bv, bn) = b.map(|d| (d.value, d.n)).unwrap_or((0.0, 0));
        let n = a.n + bn;
        // Both absent stays absent — 0/0 must not become a confident 0.0.
        let value = if n == 0 { 0.0 } else { (a.value * a.n as f64 + bv * bn as f64) / n as f64 };
        dims.push(Dim { value, n, ..a.clone() });
    }
    let mut faces: BTreeMap<String, u64> = profile.emoticons.iter().cloned().collect();
    for (f, n) in &fresh.emoticons {
        *faces.entry(f.clone()).or_insert(0) += n;
    }
    let mut emoticons: Vec<(String, u64)> = faces.into_iter().collect();
    emoticons.sort_by(|a, b| b.1.cmp(&a.1).then(a.0.cmp(&b.0)));
    Markers {
        dims,
        emoticons,
        words: profile.words + fresh.words,
        messages: profile.messages + fresh.messages,
    }
}

/// One dimension's reading, for a report. Carries no judgement — `delta` is a distance, not a score,
/// and nothing here says whether it is large.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct DimReading {
    pub name: String,
    pub about: String,
    /// The profile's value, or `None` when the profile never measured this dimension.
    pub profile: Option<f64>,
    /// The sample's value, or `None` when the sample gave nothing to measure — which is the COMMON
    /// case for a short message and must never read as 0.0.
    pub sample: Option<f64>,
    /// `|sample - profile|`, present only when BOTH sides were measured.
    pub delta: Option<f64>,
    pub sample_n: u64,
}

/// What `explain` returns: a per-dimension reading and nothing else.
///
/// There is DELIBERATELY no overall score, no verdict, no confidence and no id. Adding one is the
/// change that turns this from an instrument into a suspicion ledger, and the maintainer's stated
/// values rule that out — so it is absent from the TYPE, not merely from the current caller.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct Explain {
    /// Only ever the discriminative dimensions. The descriptive ones are shown by `show`, not here:
    /// averaging them into a comparison MEASURABLY worsened separation (overlap 537 -> 1327 of 1764).
    pub readings: Vec<DimReading>,
    /// The descriptive readings, reported alongside for a human to look at — never compared.
    pub descriptive: Vec<DimReading>,
    /// Emoticons in the sample that the profile has never seen. Not evidence of anything; the most
    /// likely explanation is that the person used a new face, which people do.
    pub unseen_emoticons: Vec<String>,
    /// The behavioural readings, when the caller supplied an occasion to read. These are reported
    /// exactly like the descriptive linguistic dimensions — mass, never a judgement — and for the
    /// same reason: they have not yet earned discriminative status by measurement.
    #[serde(default)]
    pub behaviour: Vec<crate::behaviour::DistReading>,
    /// False when the profile rests on too little corpus for any of this to mean anything.
    pub profile_evidenced: bool,
    pub profile_words: u64,
    pub sample_words: u64,
}

fn reading(p: Option<&Dim>, s: Option<&Dim>) -> DimReading {
    let pv = p.filter(|d| d.measured()).map(|d| d.value);
    let sv = s.filter(|d| d.measured()).map(|d| d.value);
    DimReading {
        name: p.or(s).map(|d| d.name.clone()).unwrap_or_default(),
        about: p.or(s).map(|d| d.about.clone()).unwrap_or_default(),
        profile: pv,
        sample: sv,
        delta: match (pv, sv) {
            (Some(a), Some(b)) => Some((b - a).abs()),
            _ => None,
        },
        sample_n: s.map(|d| d.n).unwrap_or(0),
    }
}

/// Compare a sample against a profile, per dimension.
///
/// Reads the discriminative dimensions THROUGH [`Markers::discriminative`], which is the only way to
/// obtain them — so a future caller cannot quietly fold a descriptive dimension back in.
///
/// `occasion` is when and where the sample happened, when the caller knows. It is OPTIONAL because
/// the linguistic half must keep working on a bare string — `bsc imprint explain "<text>"` has no
/// occasion, and a missing one costs the behavioural readings rather than the call.
pub fn explain_at(profile: &Profile, sample: &Markers, occasion: Option<&crate::behaviour::Event>) -> Explain {
    let mut e = explain(profile, sample);
    if let Some(ev) = occasion {
        e.behaviour = crate::behaviour::explain_behaviour(&profile.behaviour, ev);
    }
    e
}

/// The linguistic half alone — see [`explain_at`] for the behavioural readings too.
pub fn explain(profile: &Profile, sample: &Markers) -> Explain {
    let readings = profile
        .markers
        .discriminative()
        .map(|d| reading(Some(d), sample.get(&d.name)))
        .collect();
    let descriptive = profile
        .markers
        .dims
        .iter()
        .filter(|d| d.kind == Kind::Descriptive)
        .map(|d| reading(Some(d), sample.get(&d.name)))
        .collect();
    let seen: std::collections::BTreeSet<&str> =
        profile.markers.emoticons.iter().map(|(f, _)| f.as_str()).collect();
    let unseen_emoticons =
        sample.emoticons.iter().filter(|(f, _)| !seen.contains(f.as_str())).map(|(f, _)| f.clone()).collect();
    Explain {
        readings,
        descriptive,
        behaviour: Vec::new(),
        unseen_emoticons,
        profile_evidenced: profile.evidenced(),
        profile_words: profile.markers.words,
        sample_words: sample.words,
    }
}
